# Calibration OUTPUTS.
#
# `selection.json`, `summary.json` and `manifest.json` ARE committed: the protocol requires
# the selection to be frozen and committed before the sealed window may be opened, and the
# other two are the published result.
#
# `trajectory.json` is NOT — it is ~13 MB of per-bar detail, fully regenerable from the
# bundle plus this commit, and it would add a fresh 13 MB blob to history on every run. The
# manifest still records its SHA-256, so a regenerated trajectory can be checked against the
# one that produced these numbers. That makes the hash a verification rather than a duplicate.
out/**/trajectory.json

# Mandate-framing EXPERIMENT artefacts — never committed.
#
# They carry the model's full responses (reasoning, theses) and the per-asset
# allocations of real production contexts. The committed report
# (docs/RAPPORT-EXPERIENCE-MANDAT-V5.md) only ever holds aggregates and hashes.
out/mandate-experiment/

# Exposure OBSERVATION artefacts — never committed.
#
# They are a read-only extract of the live journal: real allocations, real movements, real
# book. Regenerable at any time from the same cutoff (that is the point of the manifest), so
# committing them would buy nothing and would put an operational export in the history of a
# public repository.
out/exposure-observation/

# Exposure-BAND bite artefacts — never committed.
#
# Same nature and same reason as the observation artefacts above: a read-only extract of the
# live journal (real targets, real book, real freezes), regenerable at any time from the same
# corpus. The published deliverable is the report on stdout and the aggregates in
# docs/RAPPORT-MORSURE-BANDE.md, never the per-cycle export.
out/exposure-band-bite/
